installatore-apk (1.3.0-1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Replace the Fedora RPM with install.sh inside the source archive, and
    drop build-rpm.sh and the RPM spec.
  * Add Arch Linux packaging through PKGBUILD and Fedora packaging
    through an RPM spec plus build-rpm.sh, installing the same layout.
  * Make the launcher look for the application in the Fedora and Arch
    locations and suggest the right package manager when dependencies
    are missing.
  * Restore the emoji headings in the README.
  * Remove the icon from the "Installa" button: system-software-install-symbolic
    renders as a rectangle with lines inside (a SIM card / a book) in the
    default icon theme, and it was the symbol left in the orange button.
  * Show the "Installa" label in the orange box during the install, and
    drop the download icon entirely together with its assets and drawing
    code, so no icon theme can substitute an unrelated symbol.
  * Show the running version in the window, so it is immediately visible
    an unrelated symbol depending on the installed theme. The indicator now
    resolves SVG, then PNG, then a Cairo drawing, so it can never end up
    showing a stock icon.

 -- Installatore Apk <installatore-apk@localhost>  Fri, 25 Sep 2026 00:00:00 +0000
    whether an old installation is still being launched.
  * Load the download glyph from SVG through librsvg, keeping the PNG as
    a fallback when the SVG loader is unavailable.
  * Ship the orange download glyph as a PNG and load it directly, instead
    of relying on theme icon names: depending on the icon theme the box
    could show an unrelated symbol. The PNG needs no SVG loader.
  * Remove the previously built .deb files on every build, so dist/ only
    ever holds the current package.
  * Stop asking for administrator authentication on every launch: the
    launcher no longer installs anything and never calls pkexec, it only
    reports the missing packages. It was prompting for a password on each
    start because the SVG loader check could never pass (it needed a
    GdkPixbuf typelib that the launcher did not install).
  * Run apt-get from postinst only when something is genuinely missing.
  * Use the standard GNOME download icon (folder-download-symbolic) in
    the orange installation indicator.
  * Report a clear message, with the one-time plugdev command, when USB
    access is denied, so the app never needs root to work.
  * Use a standard GTK4 download icon for the installation indicator
    instead of a hand-drawn one, picking the first name available in the
    current icon theme.
  * Stop tracking the generated dist/ folder in git and add a .gitignore
    for build artefacts.
  * Drop python3-cairo from the dependencies, no longer needed.
  * Draw the download indicator with Cairo instead of loading an SVG, so
    the orange tray-and-arrow icon shows even without the librsvg loader
    (the standard GNOME icon used to appear in its place).
  * Redraw the download indicator as a readable orange outline (tray plus
    downward arrow): the previous artwork filled the whole 40x29 canvas
    and rendered as a solid orange block.
  * Add translated package descriptions (it, en, de, fr, es, pt) so the
    software centre shows the text matching the system language, keeping
    the Italian title in every variant.
  * Install librsvg2-common (SVG loader) so the app icon and the download
    indicator actually render instead of showing an empty image.
  * Detect a failed texture load and fall back to a symbolic icon.
  * Use the requested orange download icon inside an orange rounded
    square for the installation progress indicator.
  * Add the installatore-apk CLI: without arguments it uses the single
    .apk file found in the current directory.
  * Show the app icon in the header bar and match the desktop entry to
    the com.simonecompany.installatoreapk application id so the system
    bar no longer shows a generic icon.
  * Show a green check with "Fatto!" when the installation completes.
  * Remove the project website link from the window and the README.

